2095:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * takes extra precautions against adversaries who can observe memory
2096:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * access patterns.
2097:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      *
2098:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * This function performs a series of multiplications by table elements and
2099:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * squarings, and we want the prevent the adversary from finding out which
2100:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * table element was used, and from distinguishing between multiplications
2101:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * and squarings. Firstly, when multiplying by an element of the window
2102:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * W[i], we do a constant-trace table lookup to obfuscate i. This leaves
2103:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * squarings as having a different memory access patterns from other
2104:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * multiplications. So secondly, we put the accumulator in the table as
2105:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * well, and also do a constant-trace table lookup to multiply by the
2106:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * accumulator which is W[x_index].
2107:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      *
2108:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * This way, all multiplications take the form of a lookup-and-multiply.
2109:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * The number of lookup-and-multiply operations inside each iteration of
2110:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * the main loop still depends on the bits of the exponent, but since the
2111:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * other operations in the loop don't have an easily recognizable memory
2112:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * trace, an adversary is unlikely to be able to observe the exact
2113:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * patterns.
2114:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      *
2115:Libs/mbedtls/library/bignum.c ****      * An adversary may still be able to recover the exponent if they can
